ns.\n- Additionally, I8 is on the diagonal with H9 and G10: I8-H9-G10 — all would be O.\n\nYes! I8, H9, G10 — that’s a diagonal going down-left.\n\nAnd the next stone F11 would make four, then E12 five.\n\nSo after playing I8, we have:\n- A four-in-a-row on row 8: immediate lethal threat\n- An open three on the diagonal I8-H9-G10\n\nThis is a double threat: if Black blocks the horizontal at J8, we can build the diagonal.\n\nMoreover, the diagonal is open on both ends: I8 is new, G10 is existing, and we can extend to F11, etc.\n\nBut more importantly, **after I8, the horizontal four must be blocked at J8 or E8**.\n\nBut E8 is also interesting: E8 would extend the other way.\n\nBut the point is: **playing I8 creates an immediate four-in-a-row threat**, which is stronger than just extending a three to a four.\n\nWhereas playing G7 or G11 only creates a four-in-a-row threat.\n\nBut playing I8 creates a four-in-a-row threat as well.\n\nAnd it uses the existing three on row 8.\n\nRow 8: F8,G8,H8 are O — yes.\n\nSo playing I8 makes four: F8-G8-H8-I8.\n\nThen J8 would complete five.\n\nSo this is a direct win threat.\n\nSimilarly, playing E8 would also make four: E8-F8-G8-H8.\n\nWhich is better?\n\nE8 vs I8.\n\nE8: col E, row 8.\n\nIs E8 adjacent to anything?\n\nD8? C8? Not occupied.\n\nF8 is O.\n\nBut also, E8 is diagonal to F9? F9=X.\n\nOr to D7? Not relevant.\n\nI8 is adjacent to I9, which is X (Black).\n\nSo I8 is next to I9=X.\n\nSimilarly, E8 has no adjacent enemies.\n\nBut both are valid.\n\nHowever, if we play I8, and Black plays J8 to block, then we can play elsewhere.\n\nBut the threat is the same.\n\nBut notice: if we play I8, the open end is J8.\n\nIf we play E8, the open end is D8.\n\nJ8 is farther from other stones.\n\nBut more importantly, is there a difference in follow-up?\n\nAfter I8, we have the diagonal I8-H9-G10.\n\nAfter E8, we have no such bonus.\n\nSo **I8 is better because it participates in two lines:**\n- Horizontal: F8-G8-H8-I8 (four)\n- Diagonal